spans.s:
Māgandika,
(1) = Mākandika (Pali Māgandiya): in the ‘Skt. Suttanipāta’, Hoernle, JRAS 〔1916.714—5〕;
(2) as common noun, in Mv 〔iii.412.7〕 (prose), applied to some kind of heretical (brahmanical) ascetics or sectarians, in a cpd., after parivrājakā, traidaṇḍika-mānandika-(so mss., read māga˚, or māka˚?)-guruputraka-gautama- etc.; cf. Pali AN 〔iii.276.32 f.〕, similar list, paribbājako … māgaṇḍiko … tedaṇḍiko … aviruddhako … gotamako, etc.; the word māgaṇḍika (note ṇḍ) is not recorded in PTSD or Childers; it seems doubtful whether it is related to the n. pr. Māgandika (1).
